        "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Few Terms of an Expansion With Negative Coefficients",
        "problem": "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${( x - 3y )}^ 5.$",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5,$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-3y ,$ and $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5-1}(-3y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5-2}(-3y)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1(x)^{5}+5(x)^{4}(-3y)+10(x)^{3}(9{y}^{2})+\u22ef \\\\  & ={x}^{5}-15{x}^{4}y+90{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$",
